\label{ch:memory-management}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:mm-intro}

Memory management---the allocation and reclamation of memory during program execution---is one of the most consequential decisions in language design. The choice of memory management strategy affects performance, safety, programmability, and the kinds of programs that can be written. This chapter surveys the major approaches to memory management, from the simplest manual schemes to the most sophisticated compile-time analyses.

Every object allocated during program execution must eventually be reclaimed. The central question is: who decides when, and how? In manual management, the programmer decides. In garbage collection, the runtime decides. In ownership-based systems, the compiler decides at compile time. Each approach has distinct trade-offs in safety, performance, and programmer burden.

\section{Manual Management: malloc/free}\label{sec:manual}

\subsection{The Explicit Contract}\label{sec:manual-contract}

In manual memory management, the programmer explicitly allocates memory (via \texttt{malloc}, \texttt{new}, etc.) and explicitly frees it (via \texttt{free}, \texttt{delete}, etc.).

\begin{definition}[Manual Memory Management]\label{def:manual-mm}
\emph{Manual memory management} is a strategy in which the programmer is responsible for:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Allocating memory for each object before use.
  \item Deallocating memory when the object is no longer needed.
  \item Ensuring that no reference to deallocated memory is used (no dangling pointers).
  \item Ensuring that each allocated block is freed exactly once (no double-free).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Common Errors}\label{sec:manual-errors}

Manual memory management is error-prone. Common mistakes include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory leaks}: failing to free memory that is no longer needed.
  \item \textbf{Dangling pointers}: accessing memory after it has been freed.
  \item \textbf{Double free}: freeing the same memory twice.
  \item \textbf{Use after free}: a variant of dangling pointers where the freed memory has been reallocated for a different purpose.
  \item \textbf{Buffer overflows}: writing beyond the bounds of an allocated block.
\end{itemize}

These errors are responsible for a large fraction of security vulnerabilities in C and C++ programs. Studies have shown that approximately 70\% of serious Microsoft security bugs are memory safety issues.

\subsection{Allocator Implementation}\label{sec:allocator-impl}

The \texttt{malloc} implementation must manage a collection of free blocks:

\begin{definition}[Free List]\label{def:free-list}
A \emph{free list} is a data structure (typically a linked list or tree) maintained by the allocator that records the locations and sizes of all currently free memory blocks.
\end{definition}

Modern allocators use sophisticated strategies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Slab allocation}: pre-allocate pools of fixed-size objects for common sizes.
  \item \textbf{Size-segregated free lists}: maintain separate lists for different size classes (e.g., jemalloc, tcmalloc).
  \item \textbf{Thread-local caches}: each thread maintains its own free list to avoid contention.
  \item \textbf{Memory-mapped regions}: large allocations are served directly from the OS via \texttt{mmap}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reference Counting}\label{sec:refcounting}

\subsection{The Basic Idea}\label{sec:rc-basic}

Reference counting tracks the number of references to each object. When the count drops to zero, the object is immediately deallocated.

\begin{definition}[Reference Counting]\label{def:refcounting}
\emph{Reference counting} is a memory management strategy in which each object maintains a count of the number of active references to it. When an object's reference count reaches zero, it is immediately deallocated.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Basic Reference Counting}
\label{alg:refcounting}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{On assignment} $x \gets y$:
\If{$y \neq \text{null}$}
  \State $y.\text{refcount} \gets y.\text{refcount} + 1$
\EndIf
\If{$x \neq \text{null}$}
  \State $x.\text{refcount} \gets x.\text{refcount} - 1$
  \If{$x.\text{refcount} = 0$}
    \State \textsc{Deallocate}($x$)
  \EndIf
\EndIf
\State $x \gets y$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Advantages and Disadvantages}\label{sec:rc-pros-cons}

\textbf{Advantages}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Promptness}: objects are freed as soon as they become unreachable.
  \item \emph{Predictability}: deallocation happens at deterministic points.
  \item \emph{Incrementality}: no need to stop the world for a global collection.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Disadvantages}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Overhead}: every pointer manipulation requires updating the count (atomic operations in concurrent settings).
  \item \emph{Cycles}: reference counting cannot reclaim cyclic garbage.
  \item \emph{Intrusiveness}: the reference count must be stored in each object (or in a side table).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cycle Collection}\label{sec:rc-cycles}

Several approaches handle reference cycles:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Weak references}: references that do not increment the count. The programmer must carefully choose which references are weak.
  \item \textbf{Cycle collection algorithms}: periodically detect and break cycles (e.g., CPython's generational cycle collector).
  \item \textbf{Ownership annotations}: the programmer annotates the data structure to indicate which references own their targets (e.g., Swift's strong/weak/unowned).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Deferred Reference Counting}\label{sec:deferred-rc}

To reduce the overhead of atomic operations, \emph{deferred reference counting} batches reference count updates:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Stack-local references do not modify the count (they are assumed to be transient).
  \item Only heap-to-heap pointer writes update the count.
  \item A write buffer records modifications; counts are flushed periodically.
\end{itemize}

This approach is used in CPython and PyPy, where the majority of reference count operations involve stack-local temporaries.

\section{Region-Based Management}\label{sec:regions}

\subsection{The Region Concept}\label{sec:region-concept}

A \emph{region} (or \emph{arena} or \emph{pool}) is a contiguous area of memory from which objects are allocated. All objects in a region are deallocated together when the region is destroyed.

\begin{definition}[Region]\label{def:region}
A \emph{region} is a named collection of memory allocations with a single deallocation operation that frees all allocations in the region simultaneously.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Region Inference}\label{sec:region-inference}

In languages like Cyclone and the MLKit, the compiler infers region annotations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Each allocation is annotated with a region variable.
  \item The type system tracks region membership.
  \item The compiler inserts region creation and deallocation at appropriate points.
  \item Region lifetimes are determined by the program's control flow.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Region-Annotated Program]\label{ex:region-annotated}
Consider a function that creates a temporary data structure:
\begin{verbatim}
  let process data =
    let region r in
      let tree = buildTree(data) at r in
      let result = analyze(tree) in
      ()  (* r is deallocated here *)
      result
\end{verbatim}
The compiler determines that \texttt{tree} is only used within the region \texttt{r} and inserts the deallocation at the end of the \texttt{let region} block.
\end{example}

\subsection{Advantages of Regions}\label{sec:region-advantages}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bulk deallocation}: freeing an entire region is $O(1)$ regardless of the number of objects.
  \item \textbf{No fragmentation}: region allocators use bump pointers, eliminating fragmentation within a region.
  \item \textbf{Cache locality}: objects in the same region are allocated contiguously.
  \item \textbf{No per-object overhead}: no reference counts or GC headers needed.
\end{itemize}

\section{Ownership and Borrowing (Rust)}\label{sec:ownership}

\subsection{The Ownership Model}\label{sec:ownership-model}

Rust's memory management is based on \emph{ownership}: each value has exactly one owner, and when the owner goes out of scope, the value is deallocated.

\begin{definition}[Ownership Rules]\label{def:ownership-rules}
Rust's ownership model enforces:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Each value has exactly one \emph{owner} at any time.
  \item When the owner goes out of scope, the value is \emph{dropped} (deallocated).
  \item Ownership can be \emph{moved} (transferred) but not copied (unless the type implements \texttt{Copy}).
  \item At any time, there is either exactly one mutable reference or any number of immutable references to a value---never both.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Borrowing}\label{sec:borrowing}

Instead of transferring ownership, Rust allows \emph{borrowing}: creating references to values without taking ownership.

\begin{definition}[Borrowing]\label{def:borrowing}
A \emph{borrow} is a reference to a value that does not take ownership. Borrows are either:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Shared} (\texttt{\&T}): multiple shared references can coexist, but none allows mutation.
  \item \textbf{Exclusive} (\texttt{\&mut T}): exactly one exclusive reference, which allows mutation.
\end{itemize}
Borrows are checked at compile time and must not outlive the borrowed value.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Lifetimes}\label{sec:lifetimes}

Rust uses \emph{lifetime annotations} to track the validity of references:

\begin{example}[Lifetime Annotation]\label{ex:lifetime}
\begin{verbatim}
  fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {
      if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
  }
\end{verbatim}
The lifetime parameter \texttt{'a} indicates that the returned reference is valid for the intersection of the lifetimes of \texttt{x} and \texttt{y}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Implementation}\label{sec:ownership-impl}

Rust's ownership model is enforced entirely at compile time. At runtime:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each value is stored inline (in the stack frame or containing struct) or on the heap (via \texttt{Box}).
  \item When a value's owner goes out of scope, its \texttt{Drop} implementation is called.
  \item Moves are implemented as bitwise copies (the source is not zeroed in optimized code).
  \item Borrows are simply pointers at runtime; the borrow checker's guarantees are zero-cost.
\end{itemize}

\section{Arena Allocation}\label{sec:arena}

\subsection{The Arena Pattern}\label{sec:arena-pattern}

An \emph{arena} (or \emph{pool} or \emph{region}) allocator is the simplest form of region-based management:

\begin{definition}[Arena Allocator]\label{def:arena}
An \emph{arena allocator} manages a large block of memory and allocates from it using a bump pointer. Individual deallocation is not supported; the entire arena is freed at once.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Arena Allocator}
\label{alg:arena}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Initialize:} $\text{arena} \gets \text{malloc}(\text{CAPACITY})$; $\text{offset} \gets 0$
\State \textbf{Allocate}($\text{size}$):
\If{$\text{offset} + \text{size} > \text{CAPACITY}$}
  \State \textbf{error} ``Arena exhausted''
\EndIf
\Let{ptr}{\text{arena} + \text{offset}}
\Let{offset}{\text{offset} + \text{align}(\text{size})}
\State \Return ptr
\State \textbf{Reset:} $\text{offset} \gets 0$ \Comment{Reuse the arena}
\State \textbf{Destroy:} $\text{free}(\text{arena})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Use Cases}\label{sec:arena-use-cases}

Arenas are ideal for:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Request processing}: allocate all objects for a web request in one arena; free the arena when the request completes.
  \item \textbf{Parsing}: allocate all AST nodes in an arena; free when parsing is done.
  \item \textbf{Game frames}: allocate all per-frame data in an arena; reset each frame.
  \item \textbf{Compiler phases}: allocate IR nodes in an arena per compilation phase.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Typed Arenas}\label{sec:typed-arenas}

Modern arena implementations (e.g., Rust's \texttt{typed-arena}, \texttt{bumpalo}) provide type-safe allocation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The arena is parameterized by the type of objects it contains.
  \item Allocations return references with lifetimes tied to the arena.
  \item The borrow checker ensures references do not outlive the arena.
\end{itemize}

\section{Comparison and Trade-Offs}\label{sec:mm-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Comparison of memory management strategies}
\label{tab:mm-comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
\textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{Safety} & \textbf{Overhead} & \textbf{Promptness} & \textbf{Complexity} \\
\hline
Manual (malloc/free) & Low & Low & Full & High (programmer) \\
Reference counting & Medium & Medium & Full & Medium \\
Region-based & High & Low & Per-region & Medium (compiler) \\
Ownership (Rust) & High & Low & Full & High (compiler) \\
Arena & High & Very low & Per-arena & Low \\
Garbage collection & High & Variable & Partial & Low (programmer) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
There is no universally best memory management strategy. The choice depends on the language's goals:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Maximum control}: manual management (C, C++).
  \item \textbf{Safety with determinism}: ownership/borrowing (Rust).
  \item \textbf{Programmer convenience}: garbage collection (Java, Go, Python).
  \item \textbf{Phase-based allocation}: arenas and regions (Cyclone, custom allocators).
  \item \textbf{Hybrid}: reference counting + cycle collector (Swift, Python).
\end{itemize}
Modern systems increasingly combine strategies: Rust uses ownership for most allocations but provides arenas for bulk allocation; Swift uses reference counting with a cycle collector; Java uses garbage collection but provides \texttt{Arena} APIs (JEP 471) for scoped memory management.
\end{insight}

\section{Hybrid Approaches}\label{sec:hybrid-mm}

\subsection{Combining Strategies}\label{sec:combining-strategies}

Modern systems increasingly combine multiple memory management strategies:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Swift}: reference counting for most objects, with a cycle collector for reference cycles.
  \item \textbf{Python}: reference counting as the primary mechanism, with a generational cycle collector for containers.
  \item \textbf{Java}: garbage collection for most objects, with \texttt{Arena} (JEP 471) for scoped memory management.
  \item \textbf{Rust}: ownership for most allocations, with \texttt{Rc}/\texttt{Arc} for shared ownership and arena crates for bulk allocation.
  \item \textbf{C++}: manual management by default, with smart pointers (\texttt{unique\_ptr}, \texttt{shared\_ptr}) for automatic management.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Case for Hybrid Systems}\label{sec:hybrid-case}

No single memory management strategy is optimal for all situations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Garbage collection is convenient but introduces unpredictable pauses.
  \item Manual management gives full control but is error-prone.
  \item Ownership/borrowing is safe and efficient but requires careful programming.
  \item Arenas are fast and simple but require phase-based allocation patterns.
\end{itemize}

Hybrid systems allow programmers to choose the right strategy for each situation, using the most convenient approach for most allocations and the most efficient approach for performance-critical code.

\section{Memory Management and the Compiler}\label{sec:mm-compiler}

\subsection{Compiler Support for Memory Management}\label{sec:compiler-support}

The compiler plays a key role in memory management:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Inserting reference count operations}: for reference-counted languages, the compiler inserts retain/release calls at every pointer copy and destruction.
  \item \textbf{Inserting drop calls}: for ownership-based languages, the compiler inserts calls to destructors when values go out of scope.
  \item \textbf{Escape analysis}: determining which objects can be stack-allocated rather than heap-allocated.
  \item \textbf{Lifetime analysis}: for region-based management, determining when regions can be deallocated.
  \item \textbf{Write barrier insertion}: for garbage-collected languages, the compiler inserts write barriers at every pointer store.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Escape Analysis}\label{sec:escape-analysis}

\begin{definition}[Escape Analysis]\label{def:escape-analysis}
\emph{Escape analysis} determines whether an object allocated in a function can be accessed outside that function. If an object does not escape, it can be stack-allocated or allocated in a short-lived region.
\end{definition}

Escape analysis enables several optimizations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stack allocation}: heap-allocated objects that do not escape can be moved to the stack.
  \item \textbf{Scalar replacement}: objects that do not escape can be replaced by their individual fields.
  \item \textbf{Synchronization elimination}: locks on objects that do not escape can be removed.
\end{itemize}

