\subsection{Arena Allocators and Fragmentation}\label{sec:arena-fragmentation}

A key advantage of arena allocation is the elimination of external fragmentation:

\begin{theorem}[Arena Fragmentation Theorem]\label{thm:arena-frag}
Within a single arena, external fragmentation is zero: all allocations are contiguous, and the entire arena can be reclaimed in $O(1)$ time regardless of the number or pattern of individual allocations.
\end{theorem}

However, internal fragmentation (wasted space due to alignment padding) can still occur. The total internal fragmentation is bounded by:
\[
  \text{internal\_frag} \leq n \times (\text{max\_align} - 1)
\]
where $n$ is the number of allocations and $\text{max\_align}$ is the maximum alignment requirement.

